WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.800
O'Neil: Mexico's transformed over the last
three decades, mostly in good ways. It's moved

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:34.570
from a closed authoritarian system to a vibrant,
if at times messy democracy. It's moved from

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:39.370
a closed economy to one of the most open in
the world and one that's increasingly competitive

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:45.500
globally. It's also seen the rise of a middle
class, and now we see a majority of Mexicans

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:49.870
who have disposable income and that in turn
is fueling economic growth in the country

00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:54.989
through consumption. So many things are moving
in the right direction. But we've also seen

00:00:54.989 --> 00:01:02.400
during this period a rise in violence, a real
crisis on the security side. In the last five

00:01:02.559 --> 00:01:09.229
years we've seen fifty thousand people killed
in drug and organized crime related violence.

00:01:09.229 --> 00:01:13.789
Much of this is led by drug trafficking into
the United States but it's become so much

00:01:13.789 --> 00:01:23.500
more. We see growing extortion, we see kidnapping,
we see contraband, we see human smuggling

00:01:23.649 --> 00:01:30.649
and these prey upon the general population
within Mexico.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:42.600
Hope: There are many drivers to this crisis.
One is government policy but not only government

00:01:42.719 --> 00:01:48.919
policy. The increase in inter-cartel violence
probably began 2004, 2005, but it was at a

00:01:48.919 --> 00:01:55.079
relatively low level. December 2006 is when
President Calderon decided to launch the first

00:01:55.079 --> 00:01:59.000
massive federal operation against drug trafficking
organizations.

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:19.950
When the government offensive began in 2007
you had basically four-five criminal organizations.

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:26.390
Over the past five years you have had an atomization
and a series of splits and schisms within

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:33.390
the organizations. And what you have is no
longer cartels but gangs.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:42.300
O'Neil: Mexico is a country that has been
historically quite unequal. You see poverty

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:47.609
in many areas. Mexico's been improving but
there are still millions that are left on

00:02:47.609 --> 00:02:54.579
the margins, and those are the types of populations
that the drug cartels or gangs look to and

00:02:54.579 --> 00:03:02.200
often recruit from. There is a popular saying
in Mexico that its better to live five years

00:03:02.249 --> 00:03:08.370
as a king, a "rey", than as 50 years as an
Ox - as a "buey". And the idea here is that

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:14.140
the opportunities for the long term for particularly
poorer youth is so limited that it's better

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:19.840
to go with the narcos now and be a narco king
than just be a laborer who would live a long

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:26.810
time and so the challenge for the Mexican
government is to turn that equation around.

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:35.300
Hope: This is also a crisis of Mexican security
and justice institutions that have shown themselves

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:42.310
quite unable to deal with this level of violence.
You have very either weak, incompetent or

00:03:42.849 --> 00:03:49.709
corrupt police forces at the state and particularly
at the municipal level. You have a judicial

00:03:49.709 --> 00:03:55.579
system where prosecutors are basically driven
to obtain confessions and not to investigate,

00:03:55.579 --> 00:04:02.579
most of the prisons are disaster areas; there
is a significant weakness in every step of

00:04:02.959 --> 00:04:07.359
the way of the local justice and security
system.

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:17.800
Vargas: In Ciudad Juarez people had undergone
a pretty extensive period of directly or indirectly

00:04:17.829 --> 00:04:26.300
being impacted by the violence, and so businesses
began to close because of the extortion. The

00:04:26.330 --> 00:04:33.330
city also lost a lot of jobs because of insecurity,
and a lot of people migrated from the city,

00:04:33.729 --> 00:04:40.620
education is really a problem and so gangs
become a very prominent phenomenon and especially

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:43.009
in more marginalized neighborhoods.

00:04:43.009 --> 00:04:50.009
Hope: The sense of fear and the sense of helplessness
has extended beyond the areas that are mostly

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:55.330
affected by the increasing violence. It has
changed the national conversation in Mexico,

00:04:55.330 --> 00:04:59.240
and it has changed the way Mexicans think
of their country.

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:04.240
Obama: There's one particular area that requires
our urgent and coordinated action, and that

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:09.860
is the battle that's taking place with -- with
respect to the drug cartels that are fueling

00:05:09.860 --> 00:05:15.960
kidnappings and sowing chaos in our communities
and robbing so many of a future, both here

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:17.919
in Mexico and in the United States.

00:05:17.919 --> 00:05:23.710
Patrick: While Mexico is unlikely to implode
and become a truly failed state, the Mexican

00:05:23.710 --> 00:05:30.139
drug war has already spilled across its borders.
In particular, it's weakened the institutions

00:05:30.139 --> 00:05:34.669
of all Central American countries, which now
suffer from some of the world's highest rates

00:05:34.669 --> 00:05:36.000
of homicide.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:51.400
Hope: The way forward is to continue dismantling
criminal organizations, strengthening the

00:05:51.449 --> 00:05:58.449
institutional framework and creating a social
and economic base for the rule of law.

00:05:58.659 --> 00:06:05.659
Vargas: I can't not be optimistic about my
country but besides security and direct confrontations

00:06:06.500 --> 00:06:13.069
to criminal activity there are a lot of things
that a society in this situation of conflict

00:06:13.069 --> 00:06:19.430
needs to address. You need to have a hard
hand but you also need to have some soft policy

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:21.750
solutions to the problem.

00:06:21.750 --> 00:06:26.520
Patrick: The international community has done
very little to try to address Mexico's drug

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:32.629
situation for two reasons. First, it really
doesn't have the mandate or the capacity to

00:06:32.629 --> 00:06:38.220
do anything, particularly without the explicit
invitation of the Mexican government. And

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:42.069
the Mexicans are very attached to the principle
of sovereignty. The second reason has to do

00:06:42.069 --> 00:06:46.940
with the United States, which has preferred
to keep its relationship with Mexico on a

00:06:46.940 --> 00:06:52.280
bilateral framework rather than going for
a more regional approach.

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:58.250
O'Neil: The United States can play a big role
in helping Mexico with the problem of violence.

00:06:58.250 --> 00:07:03.129
They could deal with the guns that are going
south and police the border much more strongly.

00:07:03.129 --> 00:07:07.789
They can help slow the money that feeds the
violence that keeps these organizations going

00:07:07.789 --> 00:07:13.550
by stopping the money laundering that goes
south to Mexico. We can also have a real discussion

00:07:13.550 --> 00:07:17.169
about demand in the United States.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:30.300
Hope: The drug war is not winnable in the
sense that drug flow will not stop but it

00:07:30.349 --> 00:07:40.900
is winnable in the sense that the country
can regain normalcy. My greatest fear is that

00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:48.800
the current levels of violence will become
entrenched -- that we'll get used to having

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:54.199
30,000 homicides a year, that the sense

00:07:54.199 --> 00:08:01.199
of urgency that there is now will be lost.

